# 2026-07-30 progress notes (task still OPEN — second half pending)
- LANDED (first half): `all <store>` is a first-class row set — a real
  Vec of the store's in-memory record structs, built by emit_store_all
  pushing each live (non-tombstoned) record through the normal vec
  machinery. Bindable, iterable, `.length`-able, passable to an
  unannotated (inferable-generic) function. The old emission returned a
  bare malloc'd array typed Ptr(Struct) with no length traveling with
  it, so `for u in all users` walked garbage and crashed. Pin flipped;
  conformance test covers empty/many/tombstone-excluded/pass-to-fn.
- OPEN (second half): queries as `Result of <row>, StoreError`. A full
  desugar (`exists ? Ok(row) ! Err(Missing)`) was implemented and
  REVERTED: the Err/miss path worked, but the Ok payload read garbage —
  `row.age` after `match r / Ok(row)` returned the first bytes of the
  name field ("Alic" as an i64). Root cause: Type::Row has a dual
  representation (fixed-256-byte record strings on disk vs 24-byte SSO
  in the jinn view), and enum-payload store/unwrap goes through a
  layout that disagrees with Row field-access offsets. Fixing requires
  teaching enum payload codegen a canonical Row representation (or
  lowering Row to the __store_N struct type before it enters a
  VariantCtor). Also: annotating a parameter `as Vec of items` (store
  name as element type) resolves to the wrong struct — user-facing row
  type naming needs a decision.
- The zero-row fabrication on a miss therefore still stands; docs
  continue to carry the "guard with count" warning until this lands.
